@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy Policy";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <h1 class="mb-4">Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4"><strong>Last Updated:</strong> November 2025</p>

    <!-- Combined Summary & Security Notice -->
    <div class="alert alert-info border" role="alert">
        <p class="mb-3">
            <strong>🔐 Privacy-First Design:</strong>

            TuneBridge is built with privacy-first principles in mind. Only the minimum data necessary to convert music
            links gets collected.<br />
            We don't track listening history or share personal data. Only the basic album or track info you submit gets
            used to query the music service APIs.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-0">
            <strong>Infrastructure Note:</strong> TuneBridge is hosted on OVH Cloud and proxied through Cloudflare.
            These third party services may collect
            limited technical data (IP addresses, request metadata) according to their own privacy policies.
        </p>
        <p class="text-muted mt-3">
            See <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                Cloudflare's
                Privacy Policy
            </a> and
            <a href="https://www.ovhcloud.com/en/terms-and-conditions/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                OVH's
                Privacy Policy
            </a> for details.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 1. Overview -->
    <section class="mb-5">
        <h3>1. Overview</h3>
        <p>
            TuneBridge is a cross-platform music link converter created and ran by <a href="https://github.com/tsmarvin">Taylor Marvin</a> that helps you share music across Apple Music,
            Spotify, and Tidal.<br />
            This privacy policy explains what data gets collected by the service, how it is used, stored, and protected.
        </p>
        <p>
            I (Taylor Marvin) am committed to your privacy and maintain strict data minimization practices. The application code is
            open source and can be viewed on <a href="https://github.com/tsmarvin/TuneBridge">GitHub</a>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 2. Data Collected by the Service -->
    <section class="mb-5">
        <h3>2. Data Collected by the Service</h3>

        <h4>2.1 Account Information</h4>
        <div class="card mb-3">
            <div class="card-body">
                <p><strong>When you create an account, the service stores:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Email address</strong> - Used as your unique account identifier and for authentication
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Password</strong> - Hashed using industry-standard cryptography, never stored in plain text
                    </li>
                    <li><strong>Account creation timestamp</strong> - When your account was created</li>
                    <li><strong>Email address verification status</strong> - Whether you have verified your email</li>
                </ul>
                <hr>
                <p><strong>For API authentication and usage tracking, the service stores:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <strong>API Key Hash</strong> - A cryptographic hash of your API key, stored securely for
                        authentication
                    </li>
                    <li><strong>API Key Salt</strong> - A random value used to hash your API key</li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Request count</strong> - Number of API requests made in the current rate limit window
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Rate limit window start timestamp</strong> - When your current hourly limit period began
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-muted mt-3">
                    <strong>Important:</strong> Your API key is only shown once when created.
                    The service stores only a salted hash, making it impossible to recover the original key even if our
                    database is compromised.
                    Rate limit data is reset hourly.
                </p>
                <p class="text-muted">
                    <strong>Note:</strong> The service does <em>not</em> collect your real name, phone
                    number, or demographic information.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h4>2.2 Connected Music Service Accounts (OAuth)</h4>
        <div class="card mb-3">
            <div class="card-body">
                <p><strong>When you connect your Apple Music account, the service stores:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Apple Music User Token</strong> - An OAuth token that allows TuneBridge to access your
                        Apple Music library on your behalf
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Token Expiration</strong> - The expiration timestamp of your Apple Music token
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-muted mt-3">
                    <strong>What we access with your token:</strong>
                </p>
                <ul class="text-muted">
                    <li>Your Apple Music library playlists (names and IDs only)</li>
                    <li>Track information within playlists you choose to process</li>
                </ul>
                <hr>
                <p><strong>What we do NOT access or store:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ Your Apple Music subscription status or payment information</li>
                    <li>❌ Your listening history or play counts</li>
                    <li>❌ Your Apple ID credentials or personal profile</li>
                    <li>❌ Playlists you do not explicitly choose to process</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-muted mt-3">
                    <strong>Note:</strong> You can disconnect your Apple Music account at any time from your account
                    settings, which will delete your stored token. Token data expires automatically when your Apple
                    Music authorization expires.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h4>2.3 Music Link Search Data</h4>
        <div class="card mb-3">
            <div class="card-body">
                <p><strong>What is collected and cached:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Input URLs</strong> - The music service links you search (e.g., Spotify URLs, Apple
                        Music links, Tidal links)
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Search metadata</strong> - Artist names, track/album titles, ISRC codes, UPC codes,
                        artwork URLs, and market regions
                    </li>
                    <li><strong>Provider links</strong> - URLs to the same music on Apple Music, Spotify, and Tidal</li>
                    <li><strong>Cache timestamps</strong> - When searches were added and last looked up</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-muted mt-3">
                    <strong>Retention:</strong> Cache entries are automatically deleted after
                    <strong>7 days</strong>.
                </p>
                <p class="text-muted">
                    <strong>Storage:</strong> Search data is stored in a SQLite database and the search results will be
                    stored as a public
                    <a href="https://atproto.com/">AT Protocol</a> records on our PDS (Personal Data Store).
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h4>2.4 TuneBridge Playlists</h4>
        <div class="card mb-3">
            <div class="card-body">
                <p><strong>When you create a TuneBridge playlist, the service stores:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Playlist ID</strong> - A unique identifier for the playlist
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>User ID</strong> - Your TuneBridge account ID (if logged in)
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Playlist Title and Description</strong> - Optional metadata you provide
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Card IDs</strong> - References to the music cards (tracks/albums) included in the playlist
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Creation and Expiration Timestamps</strong> - When the playlist was created and when it expires
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-muted mt-3">
                    <strong>Retention:</strong> Playlists created by anonymous users expire after 2 weeks. Playlists
                    created by logged-in users do not expire automatically but can be deleted at any time.
                </p>
                <p class="text-muted">
                    <strong>Note:</strong> TuneBridge playlists are separate from your music service playlists. They are
                    collections of cross-platform music links that you create within TuneBridge.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h4>2.5 Logging Data</h4>
        <div class="card mb-3">
            <div class="card-body">
                <p><strong>Is Logged:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <strong>HTTP request timestamps and response codes</strong> - For both website and API requests
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>TuneBridge User ID</strong> - Anonymized identifier for rate limiting and request
                        tracking (not email addresses)
                    </li>
                    <li><strong>Rate limit events</strong> - When rate limits are exceeded</li>
                    <li><strong>Service events</strong> - Service startup, configuration loading, and errors</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-muted mt-3">
                    <strong>Note:</strong> While processing your web request additional data may be logged including:
                </p>
                <ul class="text-muted">
                    <li>IP addresses</li>
                    <li>User agent strings</li>
                    <li>Request headers and metadata</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 3. Data We Do NOT Collect -->
    <section class="mb-5">
        <h3>3. Data We Do NOT Collect</h3>
        <ul class="list-group">
            <li class="list-group-item">❌ Your listening history from Spotify, Apple Music, or Tidal</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">
                ❌ Your playlists from connected music services (unless you explicitly choose to process them)
            </li>
            <li class="list-group-item">❌ Your music service subscription status or payment information</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">❌ Your personal name, phone number, or demographic information</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">
                ❌ Browser cookies or tracking pixels (beyond session authentication for user
                login and site preferences)
            </li>
            <li class="list-group-item">❌ Detailed analytics about your music preferences</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">❌ Third-party marketing data</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- 4. How We Use Your Data -->
    <section class="mb-5">
        <h3>4. How We Use Your Data</h3>

        <div class="card mb-3">
            <div class="card-header">
                <strong>Account & Rate Limiting</strong>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Your account and rate limiting data is used to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Authenticate you to your account</li>
                    <li>Generate and manage your API key</li>
                    <li>Enforce fair usage limits and prevent API abuse</li>
                    <li>Provide personalized account settings</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-3">
            <div class="card-header">
                <strong>Music Link Conversion</strong>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Your music link search data is used to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Convert music links between platforms</li>
                    <li>Cache results to improve performance and reduce API calls to music services</li>
                    <li>Generate OpenGraph cards for sharing in Discord, Slack, social media, etc.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-3">
            <div class="card-header">
                <strong>Connected Music Service Accounts</strong>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>When you connect your Apple Music account:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Your Apple Music token is used solely to access your library playlists</li>
                    <li>Playlist access is read-only and on-demand (only when you request to view or process a playlist)</li>
                    <li>We do not continuously sync or monitor your library</li>
                    <li>Track information is used only to convert links to other platforms</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-3">
            <div class="card-header">
                <strong>TuneBridge Playlists</strong>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Your TuneBridge playlist data is used to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Store and display your curated collections of cross-platform music links</li>
                    <li>Generate shareable playlist URLs for sharing with others</li>
                    <li>Enable playlist management (create, view, delete) through your account</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 5. Data Storage & Security -->
    <section class="mb-5">
        <h3>5. Data Storage & Security</h3>

        <h4>5.1 Where Data is Stored</h4>
        <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Data Type</th>
                        <th>Storage Location</th>
                        <th>Retention Period</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Account info, API keys, rate limiting</td>
                        <td>Local SQLite database</td>
                        <td>Until account deletion</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Connected music service tokens (Apple Music)</td>
                        <td>Local SQLite database</td>
                        <td>Until token expiration or account disconnection</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>TuneBridge playlists (logged-in users)</td>
                        <td>Local SQLite database</td>
                        <td>Until deleted by user or account deletion</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>TuneBridge playlists (anonymous users)</td>
                        <td>Local SQLite database</td>
                        <td>2 weeks</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Music link cache and search data</td>
                        <td>Local SQLite database + AT Protocol PDS</td>
                        <td>7 days (configurable)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Server logs</td>
                        <td>Local file storage</td>
                        <td>
                            Typically less than 7 days (but may be kept indefinitely for additional application
                            development purposes or as required by law)
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h4>5.2 Security Measures</h4>
        <ul class="list-group">
            <li class="list-group-item">
                <strong>Password Hashing:</strong> Passwords are hashed, making them computationally expensive to crack.
            </li>
            <li class="list-group-item">
                <strong>API Key Security:</strong> API keys are stored as salted hashes. Original keys are never stored or
                recoverable.
            </li>
            <li class="list-group-item">
                <strong>Database Encryption:</strong> All databases use SQLite's built-in encryption and can be encrypted at
                rest by deployers.
            </li>
            <li class="list-group-item">
                <strong>HTTPS/TLS:</strong> All communication between your client and TuneBridge is encrypted using TLS.
            </li>
            <li class="list-group-item">
                <strong>Access Control:</strong> Database queries use parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection.
            </li>
            <li class="list-group-item">
                <strong>No Logging of Sensitive Data:</strong> Passwords, API keys, and authentication tokens are never logged.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- 6. Third-Party Services -->
    <section class="mb-5">
        <h3>6. Third-Party Services</h3>

        <h4>6.1 Music Service APIs</h4>
        <p>
            TuneBridge integrates with the official APIs of:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Apple Music (MusicKit API)</strong> - To search for and retrieve music links</li>
            <li><strong>Spotify Web API</strong> - To search for and retrieve music links</li>
            <li><strong>Tidal API</strong> - To search for and retrieve music links</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            When you use TuneBridge to convert a link:
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>TuneBridge extracts metadata from the input URL (the album ID or track ID)</li>
            <li>TuneBridge queries the music service APIs to find matches</li>
            <li>
                The music services receive only the search criteria (the album or track id, artist/title information, ISRC, or
                UPC)
            </li>
            <li>TuneBridge never sends your email address, password, or personal data to these services</li>
        </ol>
        <ul class="text-muted">
            <li>
                <a href="https://www.apple.com/privacy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                    Apple Privacy
                    Policy
                </a>
            </li>
            <li>
                <a href="https://www.spotify.com/us/legal/privacy-policy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                    Spotify
                    Privacy Policy
                </a>
            </li>
            <li>
                <a href="https://tidal.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tidal Privacy Policy</a>
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h4>6.2 Music Service OAuth Authentication (Account Linking)</h4>
        <p>
            TuneBridge offers optional OAuth integration with music services, allowing you to connect your music streaming
            accounts for enhanced features such as playlist access and conversion.
        </p>
        <div class="card mb-3">
            <div class="card-header">
                <strong>Apple Music (via MusicKit JS)</strong>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p><strong>How it works:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>You initiate the connection by clicking "Connect Apple Music" in the TuneBridge interface</li>
                    <li>Apple's MusicKit JS SDK handles the authentication flow directly with Apple</li>
                    <li>Upon successful authentication, Apple provides TuneBridge with a user token</li>
                    <li>This token grants TuneBridge read-only access to your Apple Music library playlists</li>
                </ul>
                <p><strong>Scope of access:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Read your library playlists (names and track IDs only)</li>
                    <li>❌ Cannot modify or delete your playlists</li>
                    <li>❌ Cannot access your listening history</li>
                    <li>❌ Cannot access your Apple ID account information</li>
                    <li>❌ Cannot make purchases on your behalf</li>
                </ul>
                <p><strong>Token handling:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Tokens are stored encrypted in our database</li>
                    <li>Tokens expire automatically based on Apple's expiration policy</li>
                    <li>You can revoke access at any time from your TuneBridge account settings</li>
                    <li>Revoking access immediately deletes the stored token</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted">
            <strong>Note:</strong> TuneBridge currently only supports Apple Music account linking. Additional music
            service OAuth integrations (such as Spotify) may be added in the future, and this privacy policy will
            be updated accordingly.
        </p>

        <h4>6.3 AT Protocol</h4>
        <p>
            Media link search results are stored as public AT Protocol records on the TuneBridge Personal Data Store (PDS).
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Important:</strong> To protect user privacy the music service URLs input by end users
            (which may contain music service provider tracking IDs) are stored local to the service in a SQLite database
            and not made public on the PDS.<br />
        </p>

        <h4>6.4 Discord Bot</h4>
        <p>If you add the TuneBridge bot to your Discord server, it will:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Detect music links in messages</li>
            <li>Automatically convert and reply with links on other platforms</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            <strong>Data handling:</strong> Discord may provide user and server IDs during message processing.
            We may log partial message content (the extracted music service URL) and Discord user and server IDs for
            operational and debugging purposes.
            Discord user IDs may also be incidentally logged in association with requests that cause errors in the
            tunebridge application.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 7. Your Rights & Data Control -->
    <section class="mb-5">
        <h3>7. Your Rights & Data Control</h3>

        <h4>7.1 Access Your Data</h4>
        <p>
            You have the right to request a copy of all personal data we hold about you. Use the data download button in
            your account settings on the user page. You can export your API key at any time by regenerating it through your
            account settings. The contents of your search requests are not associated with your user so your search history
            cannot be exported.
        </p>

        <h4>7.2 Manage Connected Music Services</h4>
        <p>
            If you have connected a music service account (such as Apple Music), you have full control over that connection:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>View Connection Status:</strong> Check which music services are connected in your account settings
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Disconnect Services:</strong> Remove the connection at any time, which immediately deletes the stored
                access token
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Refresh Authorization:</strong> Re-authenticate to obtain a fresh token when the current one expires
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-muted">
            <strong>Note:</strong> Disconnecting a music service only removes the token stored by TuneBridge. To fully revoke
            TuneBridge's access, you may also need to remove the authorization from your music service account settings
            (e.g., Apple ID account management).
        </p>

        <h4>7.3 Manage Your TuneBridge Playlists</h4>
        <p>
            You have full control over the playlists you create in TuneBridge:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>View Playlists:</strong> See all playlists associated with your account
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Delete Playlists:</strong> Remove any playlist you have created at any time
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Export Playlist Data:</strong> Download your playlist information as part of your account data export
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h4>7.4 Delete Your Account</h4>
        <p>
            You can delete your account at any time. A delete button is available in your account settings on the user
            page.
            Account deletion will:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>✅ Delete your email address and hashed password</li>
            <li>✅ Delete your API key hash</li>
            <li>✅ Delete your rate limiting data</li>
            <li>✅ Delete all connected music service tokens</li>
            <li>✅ Delete all your TuneBridge playlists</li>
            <li>⚠️ Cache entries may persist for up to 7 days before automatic deletion</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- 8. Logging & Monitoring -->
    <section class="mb-5">
        <h3>8. Logging & Monitoring</h3>

        <h4>8.1 What We Log</h4>
        <ul>
            <li>HTTP request timestamps and response codes (both website and API)</li>
            <li>
                TuneBridge User ID (anonymized account identifier, not email address; may include Discord user IDs for
                Discord bot usage)
            </li>
            <li>Rate limit events (when limits are exceeded)</li>
            <li>Service startup, configuration, and error events</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>8.2 What We Do NOT Log</h4>
        <ul>
            <li>❌ Email addresses or usernames</li>
            <li>❌ Passwords or API keys</li>
            <li>❌ Full request bodies or complete search queries</li>
            <li>❌ Detailed music metadata from searches</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>8.3 Log Retention</h4>
        <p>Logs may be retained indefinitely for development or debugging purposes, and as required by law.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 9. Data Retention & Deletion -->
    <section class="mb-5">
        <h3>9. Data Retention & Deletion</h3>

        <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Data Type</th>
                        <th>Retention Period</th>
                        <th>Automatic Deletion</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Account credentials</td>
                        <td>Until account deletion</td>
                        <td>Manual (via account deletion)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>(Hashed) API keys</td>
                        <td>Until account deletion or key regeneration</td>
                        <td>Manual (via account deletion or regeneration)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Connected music service tokens</td>
                        <td>Until token expiration or disconnection</td>
                        <td>Automatic (on expiration) or Manual (on disconnection)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>TuneBridge playlists (logged-in users)</td>
                        <td>Until deletion by user or account deletion</td>
                        <td>Manual (via playlist or account deletion)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>TuneBridge playlists (anonymous users)</td>
                        <td>2 weeks</td>
                        <td>Automatic</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Rate limiting counters</td>
                        <td>1 hour (reset hourly)</td>
                        <td>Automatic</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Search cache entries</td>
                        <td>7 days</td>
                        <td>Automatic</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Server logs</td>
                        <td>Typically less than 7 days but may be retained indefinitely</td>
                        <td>None (retained for development/legal purposes)</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 10. Contact & Support -->
    <section class="mb-5">
        <h3>10. Contact & Support</h3>
        <p>
            If you have questions about this privacy policy or concerns about how your data is handled:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>For dev.tunebridge.media:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/tsmarvin/TuneBridge/issues"
                                                              target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Report issues on GitHub</a>
            </li>
            <li><strong>For self-hosted instances:</strong> Contact your TuneBridge server administrator</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <hr class="my-5">

    <div class="alert alert-light border">
        <p class="mb-0">
            TuneBridge is committed to transparency about how we handle your data.
            This privacy policy reflects our actual practices and will be updated as the service evolves.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
